# Quantum-Surreal Measurement Lab

Surreal numbers enlarge the vocabulary of outcomes to include infinitesimals and infinities. The governing question is not how small a label is, but how much normalized amplitude its branch carries. Begin with the equal-amplitude experiment.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The widget shows the **equal-amplitude obstruction**: for distinct labels $s$ and $t$ and nonzero $a$, the state $a|s\rangle+a|t\rangle$ assigns probability $1/2$ to each branch. Explore the exact computation below.

<details><summary>Reveal the two-branch derivation</summary>
Orthogonality gives $\|a|s\rangle+a|t\rangle\|^2=2a^2$. Each numerator is $a^2$, hence each normalized weight is $a^2/(2a^2)=1/2$. Standard part fixes $1/2$. The numerical magnitude of either label never enters.
</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## When a branch really becomes invisible

Now vary one amplitude relative to the other. For amplitudes $1$ and $\delta$, the smaller probability is $\delta^2/(1+\delta^2)$, which approaches zero as $\delta$ approaches zero.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the standard-part principle</summary>
A finite non-Archimedean number has a standard part equal to the unique real number infinitely close to it. Therefore a positive infinitesimal exact weight has standard part zero, while $1-\eta$ has standard part one. In a finite normalized state, finite additivity preserves total observed mass.
</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

## A discrete model with the same signature

The amplitude calculation has a lexicographic mirror. A weight $(r,k)$ stores ordinary mass $r$ and infinitesimal information $k$; standard part returns $r$. Thus $(0,1)$ is exactly present but observationally invisible.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite normalization argument</summary>
Componentwise addition preserves the ordinary coordinate. If the whole finite space has ordinary coordinate one, its standard part is one, while every purely infinitesimal atom with ordinary coordinate zero has standard part zero.
</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## Compare the two regimes

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:0}}

The core distinction is now visible: a common scale cancels under normalization, whereas a relative infinitesimal scale suppresses a branch. For background, see [surreal numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surreal_number), [nonstandard analysis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonstandard_analysis), and the [Born rule](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Born_rule).

<details><summary>Advanced boundary: unlimited sums</summary>
For an ordinary finite collection, standard part commutes with the sum of finite weights. With an unlimited hyperfinite collection, every branch can have standard part zero even though exact total mass is one. An infinite-dimensional theory therefore needs a tightness or uniform summability condition.
</details>

